19
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Let's explain first what do we mean by secure boot in the context of this presentation.

20
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
We are focusing on the ARM devices only.

21
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:44,000
The secure boot is a term used by vendors for a specific boot run feature.

22
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:51,400
Potentially, the term verified boot would fit more here, as the main goal of this feature is to verify the firmware before executing it.

23
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:54,440
The signature of the loudest firmware is verified.

24
00:01:55,520 --> 00:02:02,680
During the process, the private key is used to sign the firmware, while the public key is used to verify the signature.

25
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:07,080
In that scenario, we are assuming that the bootroom is trusted.

26
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:16,080
Although some of you may disagree with that, given that, in all cases, the boot room is a proprietary piece of code which we cannot control.

27
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:22,080
The meaning of secure boot may be different in case of different architectures or in different contexts.

28
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:27,080
Now, we will present the typical implementation of the secure boot.

29
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:33,080
The most common way of storing the public key is to write it into the SOC in a persistent manner.

30
00:02:33,080 --> 00:02:40,080
manner. Usually the F-fuse, so the electrical fuse, or other form of one-time programmable

31
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:47,080
registers are used for that pull post. The public key persistently written into the SOC

32
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:54,080
will be the root of trust of the verification chain. The first component is verified by

33
00:02:54,080 --> 00:03:00,080
the boot row using the public key fused in the SOC, while the following components could

34
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:07,080
with different keys. In this case, we will be focusing only on the first step, so only

35
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:15,080
on the verification of the first binary. The typical workflow can look as follows.

36
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:21,080
At first, we need to generate the keeper, so a private and public portion of a given key.

37
00:03:21,080 --> 00:03:28,080
In a secure location, the private key can be used to sign the binary. The public part

38
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:35,280
key is then fused into the SOC. Once we confirm that the verification works correctly we can lock

39
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:41,280
the platform so only the signed firmware can be allowed to be executed. Locking the platform is

40
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:49,760
usually also done via blowing a particular fuse. Let's also briefly discuss what does it mean to

41
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:55,200
sign the firmware binary. Usually the binary data is extended with some form of header.

42
00:03:55,200 --> 00:04:02,200
The header format is specific to the given Secure Boot implementation and it can be different in case of any vendor.

43
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:09,200
The common part is that the digital signature of the binary data is one of the main components of the header.

44
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:16,200
Now we will discuss the state of the Secure Boot feature in some of the ARM SoCs.

45
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:20,200
We will start with the IMX family from NXP.

46
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:25,500
In this case, the Secure Boot feature is called HAB, so the High Assurance Boot.

47
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:34,360
It can work with up to 5 different keys, and it supports multiple types of keys, both RSA and EC keys.

48
00:04:35,960 --> 00:04:40,920
An interesting feature is that the HAB can be used to verify more than one binary.

49
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:49,480
For example, we can use HAP API in uBoot to verify the kernel image directly using the HAP

50
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:56,840
feature and public key fused inside the SOC. So a single key could be used to verify all of the

51
00:04:56,840 --> 00:05:04,600
components in the boot chain. The application nodes describing the HAP usage are freely available.

52
00:05:05,320 --> 00:05:12,040
The most useful ones would be the AN3581 and AN12263.

53
00:05:12,840 --> 00:05:17,320
There are also lots of guides online describing how the HAB can be used in practice.

54
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:25,000
The SOC reference manual can also be obtained but it requires a free registration on the NXP site.

55
00:05:26,120 --> 00:05:30,040
The code signing tool is also available after a free registration.

56
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:36,040
It can also be used with HSM devices, which is described in another application now.

57
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:43,040
At that point, it is important to remember that the vulnerabilities in bootrope may also happen.

58
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:47,040
One of them was found in the HID a few years ago.

59
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:52,040
So when using it, make sure that your device is using the patch to bootrope.

60
00:05:52,040 --> 00:05:59,040
Next, we are moving to another SOC family from NXP, the Layerscape.

61
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:09,040
In that case, the Secure Boot is one of the features of the CoreIQ Trust Architecture, which is implemented in most of the LayerSquared devices.

62
00:06:09,040 --> 00:06:13,040
In terms of feature set, it is similar to the HAP.

63
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:19,040
The documentation status is also similar. The application notes are fully available.

64
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:32,080
There is also an excellent documentation of the LSDK, which is SDK for layer-square devices, which describes how to use the Secure Boot feature in the chapter 6.1, which is named Secure Boot.

65
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:38,280
To get the reference manual and the signing tool, the registration is also required.

66
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:48,640
Now we will talk about the Marvel Armada SoCs. The details about the Secure Boot feature in this case are not publicly available.

67
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:55,040
The SOC Reference Manual and Secure Boot application node require an MDA to get them.

68
00:06:55,040 --> 00:07:01,040
Despite the lack of documentation, you can find some useful information in the Marvel GitHub repositories.

69
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:11,040
Specifically, the trusted boot text file within the U-Boot repository provides almost step-by-step guide how the Secure Boot can be set up.

70
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:20,040
We can learn that the Secure Boot process is similar for Armada 38X and Armada 7K or Armada 8K families.

71
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:27,040
The fusing command is present both in uBoot shell and in EDK2UFI.

72
00:07:27,040 --> 00:07:36,040
And the signing tool can be found in the ATF malware repository at the tool's do-image location.

73
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:43,100
The ST has entered the Linux market a while ago with the STM32MP1 SoC.

74
00:07:43,100 --> 00:07:45,480
It also provides a secure boot feature.

75
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:51,460
Unlike others, in this case only the EC keys are supported, not RSA.

76
00:07:51,460 --> 00:07:56,360
The high quality documentation on the secure boot feature and how it can be used is available

77
00:07:56,360 --> 00:07:57,360
online.

78
00:07:57,360 --> 00:08:10,360
The key generation and key signing tools are part of the STM32Cube Programmer Toolkit, which can be downloaded after a free registration.

79
00:08:10,360 --> 00:08:21,360
Another interesting example is the Xilinx. The secure boot ingot case supports the RSA 2048 with keys.

80
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:26,820
It is perhaps one of the most open vendors when it comes to the documentation of the

81
00:08:26,820 --> 00:08:32,660
reference manual and Secure Boot documentation can be downloaded even without registration.

82
00:08:32,660 --> 00:08:37,860
The application node describes the Secure Boot process presentation in great detail.

83
00:08:37,860 --> 00:08:44,480
You could also learn more about the Secure Boot from the technical overview in the technical

84
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:53,180
reference manual which is in the chapter 32 device secure boot the next one is

85
00:08:53,180 --> 00:09:01,700
Nvidia with its integral SOC family the secure boot in that case uses the 2038

86
00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:08,180
bit RSA keys as well the online Jetpack SDK documentation provides all of the

87
00:09:08,180 --> 00:09:14,360
details you would need to implement the secure boot or the devices the keys are

88
00:09:14,360 --> 00:09:20,280
simply generated using openssl command line the fusing and signing tools are part of the jetpack

89
00:09:20,280 --> 00:09:26,680
sdk the odm fuse script is used to program the fuses while the flashing script has options for

90
00:09:26,680 --> 00:09:37,160
binary signing as well in case of the microchip we have analyzed the sa ma5 socs the secure boot

91
00:09:37,160 --> 00:09:42,600
feature is also there but the publicly available documentation is missing some details of it

92
00:09:42,600 --> 00:09:48,600
There is some more documentation which requires an NDA with microchip to obtain one.

93
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:57,600
There is also a secure variant of Samba tools which is used for code signing at FU's programming.

94
00:09:57,600 --> 00:10:04,600
Its usage is described but the tool itself requires the NDA as well to receive it.

95
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:09,600
We cannot say much about the Secure Boot in the TI Citara SOCs.

96
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:15,280
the public documents only mention that the secure boot feature is there but provide not that much

97
00:10:15,280 --> 00:10:22,240
details the high level secure boot overview is truly high level that can be treated more as a

98
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:29,120
marketing material rather than a technical documentation in order to receive more details

99
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:31,840
contact with the representatives is necessary

100
00:10:31,840 --> 00:10:40,540
Qualcomm also provides only the high-level documentation in terms of the Secure Boot

101
00:10:40,540 --> 00:10:49,720
feature. But we can learn some interesting stuff from third-party write-ups such as the

102
00:10:49,720 --> 00:10:56,260
Timesys block or the LineageOS block. For example, we can learn that the signing tool

103
00:10:56,260 --> 00:11:00,260
is called intersec tool and it requires the MDA to receive one.

104
00:11:04,340 --> 00:11:10,580
ROGchip is one of the most popular Chinese SOC vendors. There is not much documentation out there.

105
00:11:11,300 --> 00:11:16,900
The technical reference manual mentions that the secure boot and f-use features are there,

106
00:11:17,460 --> 00:11:23,300
but it is not clear how it could be used. Some more documents can be found online,

107
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:29,380
like the rockchip secure boot specification we can definitely learn from there that some windows

108
00:11:29,380 --> 00:11:38,180
tools calls secure boot tool exists and it is used for signing firmer binaries so in terms of rock

109
00:11:39,060 --> 00:11:43,380
most likely there is some kind of secure boot but it is not clear how to use it

110
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:53,060
we also couldn't find any success stories about that feature contact with vendor would be required

111
00:11:53,060 --> 00:12:02,340
in that case. Another one of the popular Chinese SOC vendors is the old winner. There are some signs

112
00:12:02,340 --> 00:12:08,740
that some form of secure boot feature is there, but possibly no one knows how to use one. Some

113
00:12:08,740 --> 00:12:14,420
details can be found online, for example in the Albion forum. Specifically one of the most advanced

114
00:12:14,420 --> 00:12:18,020
user stories can also be found there, but it got stuck at some point.

115
00:12:18,020 --> 00:12:25,020
The results of research are presented in the following slide.

116
00:12:25,020 --> 00:12:33,020
We can see that out of 11 analyzed cases, the secure boot could be used without any form of NDA in six of them.

117
00:12:33,020 --> 00:12:45,020
An interesting case is the marvel, where the documentation requires NDA, but the information and tools from the GitHub repositories should be enough to implement a secure boot flow in your device,

118
00:12:45,020 --> 00:12:50,020
maybe without fully understanding all of the details.

119
00:12:50,020 --> 00:12:58,020
Also, most vendors require at least free registration to get access to some of the documents and tools.

120
00:12:58,020 --> 00:13:05,020
Some comments to the table from previous slide, which we have already covered.

121
00:13:05,020 --> 00:13:08,020
And finally, we have reached to the summary.

122
00:13:08,020 --> 00:13:15,020
After this research, we can say that the general implementation of Secure Boot is similar across vendors.

123
00:13:15,020 --> 00:13:19,020
The main idea is to verify the image before execution.

124
00:13:19,020 --> 00:13:28,020
The public key is usually stored in the FUs registers and a specific FUs needs to be set to lock the device.

125
00:13:28,020 --> 00:13:36,020
Most commonly the RSA 2038 key type is used as the signing key.

126
00:13:36,020 --> 00:13:44,020
In some cases only the EC keys are supported and in some cases multiple key types are supported.

127
00:13:45,020 --> 00:13:53,020
In all cases the SHA-256 is used as a hash function for our digital signature.

128
00:13:53,020 --> 00:14:02,020
Usually also the firmware decryption feature is present, but it was not in the scope of our presentation to discuss one.

129
